{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AFxP7xNWt1Dv72R4TUrFc5","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd002-sys\n     2→title: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n     6→  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n     7→  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n     8→  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n     9→  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n    10→  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n    11→  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n    12→  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n    13→\n    14→  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n    15→  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n    16→  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n    17→\n    18→goals:\n    19→  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query that works on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n    20→  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n    21→  - G3: Provide a SIGPIPE handler that suppresses the broken-pipe error when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n    22→  - G4: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n    23→\n    24→requirements:\n    25→  R1:\n    26→    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    27→    items:\n    28→      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n    29→      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n    30→      - R1.3: |\n    31→          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n    32→          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n    33→          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n    34→          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n    35→          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n    36→          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n    37→          output when stdout is redirected.\n    38→      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n    39→\n    40→  R2:\n    41→    title: Extended file metadata\n    42→    items:\n    43→      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n    44→      - R2.2: |\n    45→          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n    46→\n    47→            type FileInfo struct {\n    48→                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n    49→                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n    50→                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n    51→                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n    52→                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n    53→                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n    54→                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n    55→                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n    56→                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n    57→                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n    58→                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n    59→                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n    60→            }\n    61→      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n    62→      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n    63→      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n    64→      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n    65→\n    66→  R3:\n    67→    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    68→    items:\n    69→      - R3.1: |\n    70→          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer. Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n    71→      - R3.2: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n    72→      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n    73→\n    74→  R4:\n    75→    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    76→    items:\n    77→      - R4.1: |\n    78→          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n    79→      - R4.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n    80→      - R4.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n    81→\n    82→non_goals:\n    83→  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n    84→  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n    85→  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n    86→  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n    87→\n    88→acceptance_criteria:\n    89→  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n    90→  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n    91→  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n    92→  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n    93→  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n    94→  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    95→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56d4d091-4f59-48ae-9241-88e71e65067c","uuid":"30afdc4e-53c4-4f42-a6b2-faf1c42835f1","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/90/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd002-sys.yaml","content":"id: prd002-sys\ntitle: \"pkg/sys: Syscall Abstractions\"\n\nproblem: |\n  Four utilities (ls, du, find, xargs) need OS-level capabilities that Go's standard\n  library does not expose in a portable, stable form: terminal-width queries, extended\n  file metadata (hard-link count, device and inode numbers, physical block counts),\n  and signal handling for SIGPIPE and SIGWINCH. On Darwin and Linux these syscalls\n  diverge in struct layout (st_mtimespec vs st_mtim, st_blocks interpretation) and\n  available interfaces (TIOCGWINSZ ioctl). Without pkg/sys each cmd/ package would\n  import syscall or golang.org/x/sys directly, duplicating the Darwin/Linux divergence\n  handling and making it harder to test or audit the syscall surface.\n\n  pkg/sys provides a stable, tested interface for these three capability areas. Each\n  capability is driven by at least one concrete utility requirement derived from C source\n  analysis (see dependency-map.yaml).\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Provide a portable terminal-width query that works on Darwin and Linux without cgo.\n  - G2: Expose extended file metadata (st_nlink, st_uid, st_gid, st_rdev, st_blocks, st_ino, st_dev) not available from os.FileInfo.\n  - G3: Provide a SIGPIPE handler that suppresses the broken-pipe error when stdout is closed by a downstream consumer.\n  - G4: Provide a SIGWINCH handler that calls a user-supplied callback when the terminal is resized.\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Terminal width and TTY detection\n    items:\n      - R1.1: Must provide func TerminalWidth() (int, error) that returns the current terminal column count by calling ioctl(TIOCGWINSZ) on stdout. Returns an error (not panic) when stdout is not a terminal or when ioctl fails.\n      - R1.2: Must use golang.org/x/sys/unix (the project's only external dependency) to perform the TIOCGWINSZ ioctl; must not shell out to tput or stty and must not import golang.org/x/term.\n      - R1.3: |\n          Must provide func IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool that returns true when the file\n          descriptor refers to a terminal, and false otherwise. Implementation: call\n          golang.org/x/sys/unix.IoctlGetWinsize(int(fd), unix.TIOCGWINSZ) and return\n          err == nil. Returns false for pipes, regular files, and non-terminal descriptors.\n          The caller (pkg/format ColorEnabled) is responsible for extracting the fd from an\n          io.Writer via type assertion to *os.File. Required by pkg/format to suppress color\n          output when stdout is redirected.\n      - R1.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281): ls uses struct winsize to determine column count for its default multi-column layout and for the -w/--width flag default.'\n\n  R2:\n    title: Extended file metadata\n    items:\n      - R2.1: Must provide func Stat(path string) (*FileInfo, error) and func Lstat(path string) (*FileInfo, error). Stat follows symbolic links (equivalent to os.Stat); Lstat does not follow symbolic links (equivalent to os.Lstat). Both return a *FileInfo populated from syscall.Stat_t.\n      - R2.2: |\n          FileInfo must be defined as the following named struct in package sys:\n\n            type FileInfo struct {\n                Mode    os.FileMode // file mode and type bits (from syscall.Stat_t.Mode)\n                Size    int64       // apparent file size in bytes (st_size)\n                Nlink   uint64      // hard-link count (st_nlink)\n                Uid     uint32      // owner user ID (st_uid)\n                Gid     uint32      // owner group ID (st_gid)\n                Rdev    uint64      // device ID for special files (st_rdev)\n                Dev     uint64      // device ID of the containing filesystem (st_dev)\n                Ino     uint64      // inode number (st_ino)\n                Blocks  int64       // number of 512-byte blocks allocated (st_blocks)\n                Blksize int64       // preferred I/O block size (st_blksize)\n                ModTime time.Time   // modification time (st_mtime / st_mtimespec)\n                Info    os.FileInfo // underlying os.FileInfo for os package compatibility\n            }\n      - R2.3: Must abstract the Darwin/Linux divergence in struct field names (st_mtimespec on Darwin vs st_mtim on Linux; st_blocks interpretation) so callers use a single API regardless of target platform.\n      - R2.4: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:213, 371): ls needs Nlink, Uid, Gid, Rdev, Mode for -l format; Blocks for -s; Ino for -i.'\n      - R2.5: 'Utility context — du (du.c:554): du needs Dev and Ino for hard-link deduplication, and Blocks for physical block-count accumulation.'\n      - R2.6: 'Utility context — find (find/ftsfind.c:172): find predicates (-type, -size, -newer, -perm, -uid, -gid) require Mode, Size, ModTime, Uid, Gid, Nlink from the stat struct.'\n\n  R3:\n    title: SIGPIPE handling\n    items:\n      - R3.1: |\n          Must provide func InstallSIGPIPEHandler() that installs a SIGPIPE handler causing the process to exit 0 when stdout or stderr is closed by a downstream consumer. Implementation: use signal.Notify(c, syscall.SIGPIPE) with a buffered channel of size 1; in a dedicated goroutine call os.Exit(0) when the channel receives. Must not use signal.Ignore so that deferred cleanup functions do not run on SIGPIPE (matching GNU behavior where SIGPIPE terminates silently).\n      - R3.2: InstallSIGPIPEHandler must be safe to call multiple times; only one goroutine per invocation is started.\n      - R3.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:1600-1620): ls installs a SIGPIPE handler via sigaction so that piping ls output to head does not produce a broken-pipe error message. du, find, and xargs have the same requirement.'\n\n  R4:\n    title: SIGWINCH handling\n    items:\n      - R4.1: |\n          Must provide func OnTerminalResize(callback func(width int)) that registers a SIGWINCH handler. When the terminal is resized, the handler calls TerminalWidth() and invokes the callback with the new width. If TerminalWidth() returns an error the callback is not invoked.\n      - R4.2: Must support multiple calls to OnTerminalResize; each registered callback must be called on resize. Callbacks are called in registration order.\n      - R4.3: 'Utility context — ls (ls.c:2281, SIGWINCH registration): ls re-queries terminal width on SIGWINCH to re-format output when the terminal window is resized during a long listing.'\n\nnon_goals:\n  - pkg/sys does not wrap all of syscall or golang.org/x/sys; it exposes only the surfaces required by the current utility batch.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide file-watching or inotify/kqueue interfaces.\n  - pkg/sys does not handle SIGUSR1/SIGUSR2 (find's debug output); those are deferred to the find PRD.\n  - pkg/sys does not provide process-group or terminal control (TIOCGPGRP, tcsetpgrp); those are deferred to the xargs PRD.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: TerminalWidth() returns a positive integer when called in a test with a pseudo-terminal (golang.org/x/term's test helpers or os.Pipe() as a TTY substitute is acceptable).\"\n  - \"AC2: Stat() and Lstat() on a known file return FileInfo with correct Nlink, Uid, Gid, Dev, Ino, and Blocks values as confirmed by running stat(1) on the same file.\"\n  - \"AC3: Lstat() on a symlink returns the symlink's own st_mode (type bits show symlink), not the target's mode.\"\n  - \"AC4: InstallSIGPIPEHandler() causes a process writing to a broken pipe to exit 0 rather than printing a write error.\"\n  - \"AC5: All pkg/sys code compiles on both darwin/arm64 and linux/amd64 with no build errors and no cgo.\"\n  - \"AC6: All pkg/sys code compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":95,"startLine":1,"totalLines":95}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FMTQ8x6K4Bh7bWbKumdNfy","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clear picture. Let me review what I need to do:\n\n1. Create `pkg/sys/terminal.go` with:\n   - `WinSize` struct with `Rows` and `Cols` fields (both uint16)\n   - `IsTerminal(fd uintptr) bool` - reports whether fd is a terminal\n   - `GetWinSize(fd uintptr) (WinSize, error)` - returns terminal dimensions\n\nThe task requirements say to use `syscall.Syscall` with `syscall.SYS_IOCTL` and `syscall.TIOCGWINSZ`, matching the technique in color.go lines 164-168.\n\n\nThere's a conflict between the PRD (which specifies using `golang.org/x/sys/unix`) and the task requirements (which direct me to follow color.go's syscall approach), but the task requirements take precedence here, so I'll stick with the `syscall.Syscall` pattern with `SYS_IOCTL` and `TIOCGWINSZ`. I'm verifying that `syscall.SYS_IOCTL` and `syscall.TIOCGWINSZ` are available across platforms by checking how color.go implements the same pattern, then I'll write the WinSize struct and implement the terminal functions using the same syscall approach.\n\nWriting the implementation... Writing syscall wrapper...\n\nActually, I'm reconsidering the approach here—using a struct with unsafe.Pointer is cleaner for extracting the row and column values compared to manually parsing a byte array, so I'll stick with that for GetWinSize while keeping the byte array approach for IsTerminal since it only needs to check if the call succeeds.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2626,"cache_read_input_tokens":40351,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2626},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"56d4d091-4f59-48ae-9241-88e71e65067c","uuid":"59a1930f-37cd-46b5-894b-d61765ba6eb0"}
